(a) Unlawful sales. It shall be unlawful to sell or offer to sell alcohol (meaning beer and wine) for consumption on premises without first complying with this chapter. It shall be unlawful to make delivery of any such alcohol, except inside the premises or area licensed for the sale. It shall be unlawful to sell alcohol by the drink for consumption off the premises.
(b) Applications. No alcohol shall be sold by the drink for consumption on the premises except under a license granted by the city. Application for a pouring license may be made only on behalf of restaurants located in the city, on forms provided by the city, subject to the following requirements: Comply with all requirements of section 6-2((b)—(g) and (i)-(y) above, except the bond shall be in the amount of $2,500.00, and bond for beer and wine only is $1,000.00.
(c) Annual sales. Proof of quarterly and annual food sales (with totals) of 75% or more of its total gross sales, and annual food sales in excess of $150,000.00 via presenting a sworn statement of the true and correct sales during the preceding quarter and year respectively on forms approved by the city, which reflect the gross dollar sales amount for: total sales for the quarter and year, sales of alcohol for the applicable calendar quarter and year, and sales of food items for the quarter and year or a sworn certification from applicant's CPA, based on their familiarity with and review of all the relevant documentation, that said financial responsibility requirement has been met;
(d) Supply. Proof, upon request, via purchase receipts and sales records, of ongoing maintenance of two-week supply (consistent with past sales) of the types of alcohol typically sold, to prevent undercapitalized restaurants and to demonstrate the financial viability of the restaurant, or a sworn certification from applicant's CPA, based on their familiarity with and review of all the relevant documentation, that said financial responsibility requirement has been met;
(e) Distance. The front door must be not less than 150 feet from the property line of a school ground or college campus or from a church building, or 100 yards from any housing authority property or any building used as an alcohol rehab center. This is measured by the most direct route of travel on the ground. Each application shall include a scale drawing of the location of the proposed premises showing the distances or a certificate of a registered surveyor that such location complies.
(f) Hours. No alcohol may be served before 11:00 a.m. or after 11:00 p.m. (New Year's Day will close no later than 12:30 a.m.). No licensee may serve in violation of a regulation or special order of the city, or at any time in violation of state law or regulation, or more than one hour after the restaurant shuts down the kitchen for the evening.
(g) Containers, BYOB. The sale of alcohol in unbroken packages or in any quantity for other than consumption on premises is prohibited. It shall be unlawful to bring alcohol into a restaurant or any other establishment for consumption on premises, or to charge a "corkage fee" or wine bottle opening fee or similar.
(h) Promotions. No licensee shall:
(1) Give a ticket, token or any other item that can be exchanged for alcohol on purchase of another beverage;
(2) Sale of two or more beverages for a single price or at the substantially same price as customarily charged for one;
(3) The sale of one beverage with a ticket, token or any other items redeemable for a subsequent beverage;
(4) Sale of all alcohol a customer can or desires to drink at a single price;
(5) The sale of alcohol during any special time of day or week, at prices lower than customarily charged;
(6) Encouraging purchase of a second beverage at the same time another is purchased, or before the first is consumed;
(7) Promotions which have as their primary purpose the increasing of consumption of alcohol;
(8) Discounts during certain hours, "Happy Hour" Promotions.
(9) Clearing service areas. Licensees shall remove from the customer area all alcohol within one-half hour after last call. At no time shall licensees give multiple drinks, "doubles" or similar, or provide a drink before customer finishes current drink.
(10) New restaurant; temporary licenses. A person may apply as a qualified restaurant if it has not yet been in operation for 12 months. Because it is not possible to determine annual food sales 75 percent or more of its total gross sales and in excess of $150,000.00, the city may in its reasonable discretion consider size and seating capacity and kitchen facilities and all other relevant factors to determine if it appears likely the restaurant will qualify. The city may award a temporary license not to exceed 90 days. At or before the conclusion of the 90 days, the city shall use a pro rata analysis to determine if it is a qualified restaurant.
(11) Nonprofit. A 501(c)(3) nonprofit with at least 75 members regularly paying dues may apply for a temporary three-day special event license for consumption of malt beverages and wine, plus obtain a state license. No more than six licenses may be issued to a 501(c)(3)in any one calendar year. The organization must:
a. Make application at least 14 days prior to the proposed license date(s);
b. Pay $25.00 per licensed event;
c. Not hold more than 18 days and/or nights of such sales per calendar year. A license shall only be granted for not to exceed three days and/or nights per event;
d. All sales must be for fundraising, or to directly further the purpose of the organization, or made with service of a meal;
e. All applicable sections of this chapter must be complied with;
f. Only one such license shall be issued for any one location in the city, and such location shall be stated in the application. Licenses issued pursuant to this Code section shall be valid only for the place specified in the license;
(i) Caterers. A state-approved alcoholic beverage caterer who additionally holds a valid license which authorizes alcohol by the drink for consumption on the premises may be issued an off-premises license which authorizes sale of malt beverages and wine (not distilled spirits) by the drink off premises and in connection with a specifically designated catered function, a "special event license". A "licensed alcoholic beverage caterer" means one licensed per O.C.G.A. §§ 3-4-2, 3-5-2, or 3-6-2. In addition to city requirements, such caterer must receive a state license, and comply with the following:
(1) Make application at least 14 days prior to first date of the proposed license, to include without limitation name of the caterer; the date, address, and time of the event; and the caterer's license number;
(2) Fee of $250.00 annually;
(3) All sales must be for fundraising, to directly further the purpose of an organization, or in conjunction with a meal;
(4) All applicable sections of this article must be complied with;
(5) Only one such license shall be issued for any one location within the city and such location shall be stated in the application. Licenses issued pursuant to this code section shall be valid only for the place specified in the license;
(6) All sales of malt beverages and wine in connection with an authorized catered event, shall be cash at delivery or not more than 5 days in advance of the authorized event, subject to Department of Revenue regulations. Checks and credit cards are the same as cash and subject to Department of Revenue regulations.
(7) All sales are final. No broken packages of beverage alcohol can removed or returned by the caterer from the catered event to any other location. All returns must be documented on the quantity-destination report. Under no circumstances will alcohol be returned to caterer's inventory on Sunday. Returns of unbroken packages of will be handled as no sale.
(8) Leftover broken packages of malt beverages and wine shall be the property of the event sponsor.
(9) The caterer must provide personnel needed to handle all alcohol at the event: bartending services, dispensing, and serving, providing, or furnishing alcohol. Employees must be 18 or older to handle alcohol.
(10) The caterer shall notify the State Revenue Commissioner in writing of the site of the event, including such other information as the commissioner requires, and received five working days prior to such event.
(11) The caterer will keep on file at place of business, all alcohol quantity/destination reports, local event permits and all other documents, records and reports required by law and other DOR regulations for a time prescribed by law.
(12) The caterer will notify sponsors of events of the authority of the state to enter the event for inspection and enforcement of laws and regulations pertaining to sale, possession and dispossession and distribution of alcohol.
(13) Delivery of alcohol by a caterer shall be made only in connection with an authorized event. Delivery not meeting these regulations shall be a violation and DOR regulations governing transport of alcohol shall be cause for suspension or revocation of licensee's licenses and forfeiture of bond or both by the state. All alcohol transported in violation of these regulations shall be declared contraband and subject to seizure by the state.
(14) Vehicles used by a caterer for transportation and delivery of alcohol in connection with an event will be marked only with the state license number two inches high and one inch wide. No other wordage will be permitted.
(15) While transporting alcohol in connection with an event, the caterer shall have a copy of the state alcohol license, a copy of the local catering license, the catering event permit from the city and the DOR quantity/destination report.
(16) Delivery of all alcohol to an event must be made in unbroken containers. Subsequent serving of all alcohol at the event must be by the drink.
(17) A person who willfully, knowingly sells, furnishes, or serves alcohol to a person not of lawful drinking age, knowing that such person will soon be driving a motor vehicle, or who knowingly sells, furnishes, or serves alcohol to a person who is in a state of noticeable intoxication, knowing such person will soon be driving a motor vehicle, may become liable for injury or damage caused by or resulting from the intoxication of such minor or person when the sale, furnishing, or serving is the proximate cause of such injury or damage.
(Amend. of 3-13-2018)

